From 6c8a9059bea8a0fe8efeacf28414543372171d4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 21:14:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(capability): add a validated capability registry with a
 lifecycle

Third slice: the capability system the task asks for, minus its UI.

core/capability:
- CapabilityManifest declares id/version/kind/source/minimumAppVersion/
  contractVersion/permissions/input+output fields/limits/requiredTestIds.
  SemVer is hand-rolled (compare only) rather than a new dependency.
- CapabilityValidator returns issues instead of throwing, so the screen can
  show all of them: malformed ids, duplicate ids, a contract or app version the
  APK does not implement, a COMMAND field without RUN_SHELL_COMMAND, a PATH
  field without a file permission, a closed path list, limits above the hard
  caps, a workflow naming an unregistered tool, no declared tests.
- Three permissions cannot be requested at all - READ_CREDENTIALS,
  DEVICE_FLASH, DISABLE_POLICY - because a capability that could read
  credentials, flash a device or turn the policy off would sit above the policy
  engine and defeat it.
- CapabilityRegistry enforces the lifecycle: draft -> validate -> recordTests ->
  enable -> disable, with revoke final. enable() refuses unless validation is
  clean, the declared tests passed, every requested permission was granted by
  the caller, and a non-bundled manifest was reviewed. The manifest cannot
  enable itself. Every transition is recorded with its actor.
- Untrusted source is reported as a non-blocking issue, because the review gate
  in enable() is what actually handles it.

Still absent on purpose: persistence, the capability screen, and any execution.
A workflow gets no shortcut through core/policy - its tool calls are assessed
like direct ones (documented in docs/CAPABILITY_SYSTEM.md).

Tests: CapabilityValidatorTest (16), CapabilityRegistryTest (15). 120 cases now
run green locally, including the 14 pre-existing SecretRedaction tests.
