+++ b/docs/CAPABILITY_SYSTEM.md
@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@
+# Capability System
+
+`core/capability` is the registry a capability has to go through before the app will run it. It answers
+three questions in code rather than in prose:
+
+1. is this manifest acceptable at all (shape, permissions, compatibility, limits)?
+2. has it actually been tested, and did the user grant what it needs?
+3. may the agent use it *right now*?
+
+Nothing here executes a capability. It decides whether one is allowed to exist and to run, which is the
+part the task is explicit about: "the app itself must verify permissions and classify risk; asking the
+model to behave safely is not enough".
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `CapabilityManifest.kt` | `CapabilityManifest`, `CapabilityKind`, `CapabilitySource`, `CapabilityPermission`, `FieldSpec`, `CapabilityLimits`, `CapabilityDependencies`, `SemVer`, `CapabilityTestEvidence` |
+| `CapabilityValidator.kt` | `validate(manifest, context) → List<CapabilityIssue>` |
+| `CapabilityRegistry.kt` | Lifecycle: draft → validate → test → enable → disable → revoke, plus an event trail |
+
+Tests: `CapabilityValidatorTest` (16), `CapabilityRegistryTest` (15).
+
+## The contract
+
+A manifest declares, and is checked for:
+
+| Field | Enforced by |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id` | dotted lowercase, at most four segments, unique in the registry |
+| `version` | `major.minor.patch` (`SemVer`, no dependency) |
+| `kind` | `DECLARATIVE_UI`, `WORKFLOW`, `REMOTE_SERVER`, `CORE_CHANGE` |
+| `source` | `BUNDLED` (trusted) vs `WORKSPACE_FILE` / `REMOTE_SERVER` (need review) |
+| `minimumAppVersion` | refused when newer than the installed app |
+| `dependencies.contractVersion` | refused when it differs from what the app implements |
+| `permissions` | only non-forbidden ones; a permission is *requested* here, *granted* by the user |
+| `inputs` / `outputs` | field names, duplicate detection, `PATH`/`COMMAND` fields must match the permissions |
+| `limits` | `maxDurationMs` / `maxOutputBytes` must be positive and under `CapabilityLimits.HARD_CAPS` |
+| `requiredTestIds` | at least one; the registry will not enable without passing evidence |
+| `dependencies.toolIds` | a `WORKFLOW` must name tools, and every id must already be registered |
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Three permissions cannot be requested at all**: `READ_CREDENTIALS`, `DEVICE_FLASH`,
+  `DISABLE_POLICY`. A capability that could read credential material, write to a device or turn the policy
+  off would sit *above* the policy engine and defeat it, so a manifest that asks is rejected at validation
+  time - before any UI offers the user a choice.
+- **Enabling is a gate, not a flag.** `enable()` refuses unless validation is clean, the declared tests
+  passed, every requested permission is in the caller's granted set, and a non-bundled manifest has been
+  reviewed. The manifest cannot enable itself, and the model cannot write any of those facts.
+- **Tests are evidence, not prose.** `CapabilityTestEvidence` is produced by running the declared ids; a
+  single failure keeps the capability out of service even if the rest passed.
+- **Revoked is final.** A removed capability keeps its history and can never be re-enabled.
+- **Advisory vs blocking issues.** An untrusted source is reported (`blocking = false`) because the
+  review gate handles it; everything else blocks the transition.
+- **`CORE_CHANGE` cannot arrive as data.** Kotlin, Gradle or permission changes need a new APK, so a
+  non-bundled `CORE_CHANGE` manifest is rejected instead of pretending a download could install one.
+- **Bounded, in memory.** 50 capabilities and 300 events; persistence and the capability screen are the
+  next change, and saying so is better than storing state badly today.
+
+## What this does *not* do
+
+- It does not sandbox anything: a `WORKFLOW` still runs through the same tools and the same
+  `core/policy` decision path, and it gets no privilege the underlying tool does not already have.
+- It does not fetch or execute code. There is no DEX/JAR/SO loading here and none should be added.
+- It does not persist across process death yet, and no UI reads it yet.
+
